            <div xml:id="d341">

                <pb facs="0428" n="330" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg330"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">723.2515/568: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Peru</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-03-16T17:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 16,
                                1920—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received March 17—1
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>51. Legation’s March 15, 6 p.m.<anchor n="82" xml:id="d341-f1"/> Minister for
                    Foreign Affairs informed me today situation growing most serious. Absolute
                    censorship telegraph by Bolivia; private advices over railway telegraph indicate
                    Montes in control. Trains to Guaqui being used for Bolivian Army. Peruvian
                    Government sending reenforcements to Juliaca and Puno and withdrawing steamship
                    traffic on Lake Titi[caca]. The Minister for Foreign Affairs firmly believes
                    that unless prevented, Bolivians under General Montes will create a situation
                    most dangerous to Peru and the peace of South America.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
